  "read": {
    "headline": "The two-year fell 6bp and the thirty-year set a 2002 high. Same afternoon.",
    "standfirst": "Tuesday was the first day since the hike on which the front end and the long end went different ways: job openings fell to a five-month low, consumer confidence to a 2014 low, Williams said there was 'no need for urgency', and the two-year dropped 6bp as October's hike odds roughly halved — while the thirty-year touched 5.621% intraday, its highest since 2002, and the twenty-year sits at 5.64%. Stocks drifted, the S&P 500 down 0.17% to 7,670.84 and utilities the best sector at +1.17%. Brent's front month is now December, near $96, with the November contract expiring today near $103; WTI settled 3.5% lower at $89.38 on recovering Saudi exports. Overnight Australia's CPI printed 4.0% and the Aussie fell through 70 cents; China's PMIs went back above 50; the Nikkei rose 1.95% and the yen touched 156.38. Quarter-end today, with PCE at 08:30 ET.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The curve bull-steepened at the front and bear-steepened at the back, on the same day.",
        "text": "The two-year is 4.89%, down 0.61% on the day but still up 12.67% over a month; the three-month bill 4.25%; the ten-year 5.26%, up 10.74% over a month; the thirty-year 5.59% and the twenty-year 5.64%. Two-tens is 37bp from 32bp on Monday and bill-to-ten is 101bp, through 100 for the first time this cycle. In the funds the 1–3 year Treasury rose 0.06% and the 3–7 year 0.04% while the 20-year-plus fund fell 0.50% and is down 12.47% over twelve months; municipals lost 0.33%. The front end is pricing a Fed that might wait until December; the long end is pricing supply, a 4.6% inflation-expectations survey and a Treasury buyback programme that has not capped anything. Both can be right, which is why the spread is widening."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brent's row is December now, and the roll is over. Tuesday's fall was real.",
        "text": "The source table's Brent row has completed its switch to the December contract, $96.30 this morning, up 0.15% on the day; the November contract expires today near $103. The five-day column's −7.69% is mostly the roll, but Tuesday's settlement — November Brent down 2.6% at $102.59, WTI down 3.5% at $89.38 — was a genuine move on Saudi Arabia resuming loadings at Yanbu through the east–west pipeline and a US offer of up to 40 million barrels from the strategic reserve. WTI is $89.52, down 3.13% on the week and 11.70% over six months even after a 55.23% year; gasoline is off 7.88% on the week and natural gas 5.38%. Energy fell 0.90% on Tuesday and 3.78% over a month, the only month this year in which the sector has lagged the oil price this badly. Heating oil, up 113.86% year to date, is the one row that has not given anything back."
      },
      {
        "title": "Utilities +1.17% was the best sector on the day rates relief finally arrived at the front end.",
        "text": "Utilities rose 1.17% on Tuesday and are the only sector up on the week, at +0.51%, with health care flat; energy fell 0.90%, materials 0.75%, staples 0.52%. Technology was unchanged despite Apple falling 2.66%, with Meta up 3.24% carrying communication services to +0.26%. The month is the rates story in one column: technology +4.29% and health care +0.11% are the only sectors positive in September; utilities −5.97%, real estate −6.28%, consumer discretionary −6.38%, financials −6.41% and materials −6.81% have all lost more than the S&P 500 ETF's 0.37%. Among the factors, momentum beat the index by 0.61% and quality by 0.35% on the day and 0.77% on the week; recent IPOs beat by 0.87% as Carnival jumped 13.4% on record revenue. The VIX closed at 16.04, unchanged."
      },
      {
        "title": "Emerging markets bounced on the day and Europe did not. The month still belongs to the dollar.",
        "text": "Korea rose 1.92%, Brazil 0.72%, South Africa 0.59% and the EM ETF 0.30% on Tuesday while France fell 1.15%, Poland 1.03%, the UK 0.89% and Germany 0.55%; the developed ex-US fund lost 0.48%. Over a month the damage is where the dollar went: Turkey −13.84%, South Africa −8.01%, Indonesia −7.07%, Mexico −6.09%, India −5.55%, Australia −5.50%; the Netherlands, up 0.37%, is the only European row positive in September. Korea is still up 133.58% over twelve months and Taiwan 79.36%. This morning the Nikkei is up 1.95% on SoftBank and the chipmakers, Taiwan 1.12% and Australia 1.02% after its CPI; the Kospi is flat. The country column is Tuesday's dollar close and quarter-end rebalancing flows land on top of it today."
      },
      {
        "title": "The euro's worst month in 14 against the dollar; the yen's best quarter in longer.",
        "text": "EUR/USD is 1.13, down 2.40% over a month and 3.55% on the year, after touching 1.1312 on Tuesday, its lowest since May 2025. The dollar gained 3.18% on the franc over a month to a 16-month high, 6.31% on the peso, 4.56% on the kiwi, and 2.68% on the Australian dollar, which fell through 70 cents this morning after a soft monthly core CPI. USD/JPY at 157.00 is the exception: down 1.71% over a month and 3.43% over three, the only major that gained on the dollar in the quarter, after a week of coordinated warnings from Tokyo and Washington and a BoJ minutes debate about hiking faster; it touched 156.38 this morning. The won is 12.67% stronger over three months. In crypto Bitcoin is $83,424, flat on the day and down 26.86% over twelve months; Chainlink is still up 96.25% over three months while Litecoin has given back 7.26% on the week."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold bounced 0.72% from a seven-week low. Cotton went limit-down.",
        "text": "Gold is $4,210, up 0.72% after Tuesday's 1.6% rebound from the lowest since 5 August, still down 6.06% over a month and 4.03% on the year while 8.70% higher over twelve months; silver at $61.45 is down 8.28% over a month and 15.60% year to date; platinum −4.16% on the month. The rebound came with the front end of the Treasury curve, not the dollar — a two-year at 4.89% instead of 4.96% is the kind of relief a metal that pays nothing notices. Copper fell 0.44% and is 0.92% lower on the month, its first negative month in a while, yet 36.44% higher over a year. In the crops, cotton fell 4.65% to its daily limit and is off 15.33% over a month, cocoa lost 3.24% and 20.13% over a month, and sugar is the outlier at +7.66% on the week."
      }
    ]
  },
